evaluations.strengths:
It targets the best-supported regularity in the brief: WML7 +0.36 to +0.55 across three regimes, REV −0.46 to −0.57 (t ≈ −3), 1d IC +0.026 (t 3.8). It correctly reads this as a ~1-day effect with a weak tail (alpha_tail 0.25, H_e 2 d). The framing is honest (prior ≈1/3, central ≈0). Mechanics are sound:
- explicit cost gate with λ-shrunk quadratic sizing;
- adaptive minimum hold and a turnover cap;
- single-fee atomic rotation (fee-once rule verified; τ0.00125 vs 0.00186);
- a trailing stop on executable rather than spot value.
The falsification battery is good: lag-aware IC (F1 requires IC(300)/IC(0) ≥ 0.5), thin-universe test F2 with power stated, horizon decay F3, block-level latency study F14, permutation, lagged-signal and random-entry placebos F10, P&L attribution F15 to unmask disguised carry, capacity curve F12, and ablation ladder F8.

Its default position is zero, matching the brief's strongest negative evidence (−68% by d90, 0/11 positive). The Gatekeeper is useful shared infrastructure that every sleeve and the overlay need. It provides:
- a generation registry built from NetworkRegistrationQueued about 20 blocks before NetworkAdded;
- seed-state reconstruction, an immunity calendar and a registration clock;
- mechanical flags.
The mechanics are checked: the lock-cost path reproduces 1,003.01, and the EMA warm-up is right. The injection-cap analysis correctly kills the enable-event trade (enabling a young high-rp launch adds depth, not a bid), and the decay ODE is presented only as a plausibility check. It is candid about untestability: 2 right-tail winners, FT4 'not testable' below 8 trades, Fisher p-value bounds, P1/P2/P3 strata. FT1 (total-return restatement) and FT2 (early-life realised yield) are cheap, high-information offline tests that also feed flow-yield.

It targets the loss channels that dominate micro-cap P&L and are mechanically forecastable. On pruning:
- deterministic target rule (52/52);
- t* kinematics and the network-wide 14,400-block hazard-zero window;
- empirical hazard indexed by lock-cost ratio, Monte Carlo P_prune, and recovery formula R (0.368 matches SN92);
- Tier A/B/backstop exits with an out-of-sample replay on prunes 27–52, including precision and ablation.
On valuation, sizing and cost:
- liquidation-marked NAV (one-shot sim_sell) avoids the self-impact spot mark;
- it correctly sees that CPMM tranching is path-independent, so only chain-buy refill justifies tranches;
- pool caps use stressed T, sizing is impact-optimal V*, and the minimum order is set against fixed fees;
- dust and remainder rules, plus the alpha-fee-trap buffer.
On execution and safety:
- delegate rotation for nonce lockout and ProxyExecuted reconciliation;
- key-compromise alarms, accepting only a Staking proxy and refusing Any or NonTransfer;
- validation suite V1–V7 on spec change, and handling of the SafeMode-end prune race.
On portfolio controls:
- per-netuid aggregation across sleeves, with netting plus un-netted P&L for kill switches;
- a bootstrap-calibrated false-kill rate (FT8) and drills (FT11).
It respects that nothing executes live.
